You are filling gaps in a real-time spatial data ingestion system. The system consumes REST APIs (JSON, CSV, TXT) and maps them to a flat 72-byte sample array `[x, y, z, val, extent, t, ttl, tau, force_type]` where every active sample × field is a sensor presence in ICRS space.

## Source Format
Every source follows this template:
```
source <name_with_underscores>
ttl <seconds>          # re-fetch interval
force <em|gravity|acoustic|seismic-body|seismic-surface|thermal|diffusion>
url <api_endpoint_with_{lat}_{lon}_{today}_template_vars>
[lat/lon/terra]        # static position, or terra <scale> for earth-fixed
[header X-Key {val}]   # auth headers
[map <path>]           # iterate JSON array at path
[cmap <path>]          # celestial map
[lat_key <path>]       # latitude in response
[lon_key <path>]       # longitude
[ra_key/dec_key]       # ICRS right ascension/declination
[plx_key/dist_key/z_key] # distance via parallax/distance/redshift
[field <path> <name>]  # extract single value
[field_in <key> <name>] # extract from map iteration
[path <path> <name>]   # extract nested path
[format text]          # plain text, not JSON
[rows]                 # line-by-line iteration
```

## Rules
- NO auth/private keys — every source must work with public APIs or DEMO_KEY (NASA only). Skip endpoints requiring registration.
- Point-cloud compatible: sources with `map`+`lat_key/lon_key` or `map`+`ra_key/dec_key` plus distance → PC tagged.
- Force types: choose based on physical domain (seismic-body for earthquakes, em for EM radiation, gravity for mass, thermal for temperature, acoustic for pressure/sound, diffusion for concentrations).
- TTL: 60-300 for real-time (earthquakes/flares), 600-3600 for hourly, 86400 for static catalogs.
- Template variables available: `{lat} {lon} {lat_min} {lat_max} {lon_min} {lon_max} {today} {yesterday} {tomorrow} {year} {month} {day} {hour} {nasa_key}`.
- Use `map .` when the root is the array.

